## 1. The headline discipline — divergence, not rot

**Read this before changing a single gate string.**

The tests exist because our products already pass them when the product is behaving. So when a
test fails on the IE520, the default hypothesis is **not** "the test is stale." It is "this
platform emits different output, and that is a finding."

On 2026-08-12 the 26 edits made to get the IE520 running were re-audited against a control:
**12 of them (5 defects) were accommodations of IE520 verbiage** — product questions dressed up
as test fixes — and only 14 were genuine cross-platform test bugs. Two items previously recorded
as "gate-string rot" turned out to be live on every other platform.

**The control corpus:**

```
raw-data/test_scripts/5700_bootloader/
```

A complete x230v2 run of the same 5700 suite (2026-02-19, `awplus_main-20260218-1237`), with
pristine scripts **and** full console captures. Baseline: 2003 = 13 PASS / 0 FAIL, 2005 = 8 PASS /
0 FAIL, 2002 = 16 PASS / 3 FAIL / 7 UNSUP. Effectively all-pass. **Read-only — never write here.**

**The procedure, every time a gate fails:**

1. `grep -a` the string in the **x230v2** console logs (`swi_a_2*.log`).
2. Live there → **IE520 divergence. Escalate; do not widen the gate.**
3. Absent there too → genuine rot, and fixing it is correct.
4. Either way, filter framework echoes: drop lines matching `^\d{4}-\d\d-\d\d \d\d:\d\d:\d\d: `,
   because the framework logs its own `strList` and you will otherwise match *our* text as if it
   were device output.

> ### ⚠️ `grep` reads these console logs as BINARY
> `swi_a_*.log` on both platforms contain control bytes. Plain `grep` prints nothing and `grep -c`
> returns `0` — **indistinguishable from a real absence.** Always `grep -a`. This produced a false
> "0 occurrences on both platforms" mid-session before it was caught.

**The known IE520 divergences** (all five are open product questions, not settled):

| # | Other platforms emit | IE520 "9.1.0" emits | on the "pauld" build |
|---|---|---|---|
| D1 | `Verifying release... OK` + `Booting...` (95 device lines on x230v2) | raw U-Boot/FIT — `Probing SPI flash... Complete`, `Booting image 04000000#IE520-28GSX ...`, `Verifying Hash Integrity ... sha1+ OK`. Zero `Verifying release`. | ❌ **still broken — now SILENT** |
| D2 | `Reading filesystem...` → straight to `Listing of usable files` | dumps the raw filesystem listing (with sizes) in between — which is *why* the size-as-menu-index parse bug bit here and not there | ✅ fixed |
| D3 | `Saving settings... Complete` contiguous | split by `Saving Environment to SPIFlash...` (0 on x230v2, 142 on IE520) | ✅ fixed |
| D4 | `Restoring default settings... Complete` contiguous | same SPIFlash interleave | ✅ fixed |
| D5 | `Verifying release... Error: This release file is not intended for this device.` | no such stage — `Could not find configuration node` / `ERROR -2: can't get kernel image!` (upper case, so a `'Error'` keyword misses) | ❌ **still broken — WORSE, see below** |

> ### ⚠️ THERE ARE TWO IE520 BOOTLOADERS IN PLAY — ESTABLISH WHICH ONE FIRST
> `show system` → `Bootloader version :`. Everything above depends on it.
>
> - **`9.1.0`** — leaks raw U-Boot; console banner is `U-Boot 2025.01-04843-…`.
> - **`pauld`** — `IE520-bootloader-pauld.kwb`, a developer `-dirty` build (tb470:/tftproot,
>   2026-08-12), still U-Boot 2025.01 underneath but with the leak **fully suppressed**; banner is
>   `Bootloader pauld loaded`. **Not a released fix** — do not assume it is what ships.
>
> **A0 is confirmed and fixed on the pauld build.** All 11 leaked strings measure 0
> (`U-Boot 2025.01` 995→0, `Verifying Hash Integrity` 495→0, `Saving Environment to SPIFlash`
> 142→0), and the AT banner form is restored. So the leak was a *suppression* problem, fixable
> without changing the U-Boot base.
>
> **But suppression alone was not the fix.** Removing the noise did not add the AT messages the
> tests gate on. D1 (success path) and D5 (failure path) are now **silent rather than wrong**, and
> that is the single remaining ask: restore `Verifying release... OK` and
> `Error: This release file is not intended for this device.`
>
> **D5 on the pauld build is worse than on 9.1.0.** A foreign release is correctly refused but with
> *no output at all* — silent countdown, reset, normal reboot. The reboot then prints
> `Allied Telesis Inc.` / `Mounting` / `Initializing`, all of which are in case 30's **good**
> keyword list, so `send()` matches one and the test concludes the foreign release **booted**.
>
> **`BOOT_MARKERS` is broken on the pauld build.** 3 of its 4 markers are dead
> (`Verifying Hash Integrity`, `Booting kernel`, `Starting kernel` all 0); only `login:` survives,
> which is the last line of a completed boot — so every early-detection property is gone. Use
> `Mounting` / `Initializing` (present) if revising it. Also `show system` now reports
> `Bootloader version : pauld`, which is what 2002 case 70 asserts against.

**D1 and D5 are one question:** *does the IE520 implement the AT release-verification stage at
all?* Until answered, treat every case that passes only because of a widened boot marker as
**unverified**, not passed.

Ruled out as divergence — genuinely universal rot, correct to have fixed:
`'Password Successfully updated'` (absent on x230v2 too; that gate logs `wait time reached` there
as well, so x230v2 passed by accident-of-timeout identically), and the `dir *.rel` existence-check
parse (output shape is the same on both: `flash:/mainrelease.rel`).

