## 5a. Driving the Boot Menu by hand — READ THE SOURCE FIRST

**Do not improvise this.** On 2026-08-12 I drove these menus by trial and error and burned several
hardware cycles, one wrong result, and Terrence's patience. Every rule below was already written
down in the framework and in prior `swi_a_*.log` transcripts:

> *"are you just muddling your way through the menus instead of following the Log of previous
> executions, which literally tell you what to expect at every menu?"*

**Before sending a single keystroke**, read the function that already automates it —
`ATBootLoader.py::perform_one_off_boot_from_alternate_source_with_output()`,
`__set_swi_boot_from_tftp()`, `library_5700.py::runRestoreFactorySettings()` — and the prior
`swi_a_*.log` for the same case. They give the exact sends and expects. See
[[read-the-transcripts-before-driving-hardware]].

| Rule | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Menu options and Y/N confirmations take a BARE keypress** | `swi.send('1', ...)`, `swi.send('7', ...)`, `swi.send('y', ...)` — **no** `\r`. A trailing CR is consumed by the *next* prompt: `7\r` selects "Restore Bootloader factory settings" and then answers its own "Are you sure? (Y/N)" as no, landing back at the menu with no error. |
| **Only the file-selection prompt takes Enter** | It says so — *"press enter"*. The framework sends `'{}\r\n'.format(selection)`. |
| **The TFTP dialogue is answered with `\n`** | Not `\r`. And it is **conditional** — `Run DHCP to populate default` and `Enter IP version [4\|6]` do not appear on every build, so match whichever prompt is on screen rather than assuming a fixed order. |
| **A prompt already on screen will never re-appear** | If you open the port while the device is parked at a prompt, it sends nothing further. Prime with the known answer; don't wait for text that was printed before you attached. |
| **Top-level Boot Menu `0` is RESTART, not "back"** | Know which menu you are in before sending `0`. |
| **Never let two processes touch the port** | Guard with `fuser /dev/ttyUSB0` before launching, not `pgrep` (which matches its own command line). Two of my own scripts collided and produced `device disconnected or multiple access on port?`, which I misdiagnosed as a hardware fault — `dmesg` showed **zero** USB events. |

**Stray characters select menu items.** Typing `x540-tb470.rel` at a device menu selected **YMODEM**,
because of the `4` in `x540`. It then hung until it timed out. Guessing on hardware is neither cheap
nor safe.

## 6. Bench hygiene — leave it re-runnable

The measure of a finished run is that the next person can start one. Verify and record final
state in the after-action, with `show boot`, `dir *.rel` and `show file systems`.

- **Erase FLASH, Test FLASH and the security-level cases destroy the flash filesystem as a side
  effect and do not restore it.** Any suite ending on them leaves the DUT dependent on a netboot.
  These need a re-image step built into their teardown — a menu selection is not enough. Until
  that exists, re-image manually before releasing the bench.
- **Boot Security Level must be back to `none`**, or the next run of 2002/2003/2005 is locked out
  with no way in (no framework password handling).
- **Boot pointers must name files that exist.** `Current boot image: Not set` with a missing
  `default.cfg` means netboot-only.
- **Clear USB leftovers** — 2002 case 22 leaves `---w-z-03-09-a-y---.rel` (41 MB). This class of
  leftover previously filled u4's flash and caused a silent failure.
- **Restore state in a `finally:` inside `main()`, not in `tear_down()`** — `tear_down()` does not
  run when `main()` raises, which is what caused a five-case cascade.

